    <!--
      Dashboard and sensor cards. The light scheme fixes an (L*, C*) per role and varies only the
      hue per status; night does the same with its own constants, so a status keeps its identity
      and only the ramp it sits on is rebuilt. Read the block in values/colors.xml first: the hues
      below are that file's, unchanged.

      Light                                       Night
        pill label   L* 60, on white                L* 74, on a dark fill
        pill fill    L* 93, a wash of the status    L* 24, a tint just above the card
        card stroke  L* 88                          L* 38
        gradient     L* 77 -> 88, pastel bands      L* 17 -> 28, deep bands
        accent       L* 34, dark on the gradient    L* 82, light on the gradient
        wave         L* 95, a shade off the bg      L* 10, a shade off the bg

      Every chroma is scaled by how saturated its own status is (C*/30, capped at 1). Disconnected
      is a near-neutral grey whose hue is noise at C* 4.6, and handing it the same chromatic target
      as the others turned it into a periwinkle. The scale leaves the four coloured statuses at the
      target and keeps grey grey.

      The four dark tinted surfaces (both pill fill and stroke, both gradient bands) also take a
      floor of 65% of whatever chroma their hue can hold at that lightness. sRGB's gamut narrows
      as lightness drops, and by a different amount per hue: at L* 17 aqua can only hold about
      C* 20, so the C* 14 above is most of it and reads as a saturated teal, but red can hold C* 43
      there, so the same C* 14 was a third of it. A dark, unsaturated warm is precisely what brown
      is, and critical came out looking like dried blood. The floor is only on those four because
      brown lives at the bottom of the lightness scale: the pill label and the accent are light
      colours that cannot go brown, and the wave is meant to sit a shade off the background rather
      than carry the status. Aqua is inside the floor everywhere, so normal is untouched by it.

      Warning needed one thing more, and chroma could not give it: yellow has no dark form. Every
      other hue keeps its identity as it darkens, since dark blue is navy and dark red is maroon,
      but dark yellow is brown by definition, so amber at the shared lightness read as gloom no
      matter how saturated it was. Warning's four dark surfaces therefore sit higher up the ramp
      than the others: gradient L* 25 and 38 rather than 17 and 28, pill fill 28, stroke 44. The
      lift stops where the contrasts do, which is why it is not higher still.

      scratchpad/rehue_night.py derives every value below.

      This puts each pill label at 4.9:1 or better on its own fill and ~7.7:1 on the card, each
      accent at 4.3:1 or better on the gradient, and the reading at 12:1 on the card. The two
      lowest of those are warning's, bought with the lift: the pill label is 11sp bold and so is
      held to 4.5:1, while the accent is a 24sp bold heading and a 76dp icon, large text at 3:1.
    -->
    <color name="status_blue">#FF01C8E2</color>
    <color name="status_orange">#FFDEAE62</color>
    <color name="status_red">#FFFF9B93</color>
    <color name="status_gray">#FFB4B5C2</color>
    <color name="status_green">#FF70C88B</color>

